      "text": "To solve the given system of equations \\[\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\nx + 3y &= 3, \\\\\n\\left|\\left|x\\right| - \\left|y\\right|\\right| &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\] we need to consider the nature of the absolute value equation and find the corresponding values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nFirst, let's break down the absolute value equation and consider it in different cases:\n\n1. If \\(x \\geq 0\\) and \\(y \\geq 0\\), then \\(\\left|\\left|x\\right| - \\left|y\\right|\\right| = x - y = 1\\).\n2. If \\(x \\geq 0\\) and \\(y \\leq 0\\), then \\(\\left|\\left|x\\right| - \\left|y\\right|\\right| = x + y = 1\\).\n3. If \\(x \\leq 0\\) and \\(y \\geq 0\\), then \\(\\left|\\left|x\\right| - \\left|y\\right|\\right| = -x + y = 1\\).\n4. If \\(x \\leq 0\\) and \\(y \\leq 0\\), then \\(\\left|\\left|x\\right| - \\left|y\\right|\\right| = -x - y = 1\\).\n\nLet's consider each case separately and solve the system of equations.\n\n### Case 1: \\(x \\geq 0\\) and \\(y \\geq 0\\)\nThe equations become:\n\\[x + 3y = 3\\]\n\\[x - y = 1\\]\n\nWe can solve these equations by substitution or elimination. Let's solve using elimination:\n\\[x - y = 1\\]\n\\[3(y - 1) - 3y + 3 = 3\\]\n\\[3y - 3 - 3y + 3 = 3\\]\n\\[0 = 3\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, so there are no solutions in this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\(x \\geq 0\\) and \\(y \\leq 0\\)\nThe equations become:\n\\[x + 3y = 3\\]\n\\[x + y = 1\\]\n\nWe can solve these equations by elimination:\n\\[x + y = 1\\]\n\\[3(y + 1) + 3y - 3 = 3\\]\n\\[3y + 3 + 3y - 3 = 3\\]\n\\[6y = 3\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{1}{2}\\]\n\nSince \\(y \\leq 0\\), this solution is not valid. Thus, there are no solutions in this case either.\n\n### Case 3: \\(x \\leq 0\\) and \\(y \\geq 0\\)\nThe equations become:\n\\[x + 3y = 3\\]\n\\[-x + y = 1\\]\n\nWe can solve these equations by elimination:\n\\[-x + y = 1\\]\n\\[3(y + 1) + 3y - 3 = 3\\]\n\\[3y + 3 + 3y - 3 = 3\\]\n\\[6y = 3\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{1}{2}\\]\n\nSince \\(x \\leq 0\\), we need to check the corresponding \\(x\\) value:\n\\[x + 3\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) = 3\\]\n\\[x + \\frac{1}{2} = 3\\]\n\\[x = 3 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{5}{2}\\]\n\nSince \\(x \\leq 0\\), this solution is not valid. Thus, there are no solutions in this case either.\n\n### Case 4: \\(x \\leq 0\\) and \\(y \\leq 0\\)\nThe equations become:\n\\[x + 3y = 3\\]\n\\[-x - y = 1\\]\n\nWe can solve these equations by elimination:\n\\[-x - y = 1\\]\n\\[x + 3y = 3\\]\n\nWe already have two equations:\n\\[x + 3y = 3\\]\n\\[-x - y = 1\\]\n\nAdding these equations:\n\\[2y = 4\\]\n\\[y = 2\\]\n\nSince both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are less than 0, this solution is not valid. Thus, there are no solutions in this case either.\n\nIt appears that there are no valid solutions to the given system of equations. Let's use Python to verify this result.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define variables\r\nx, y = symbols('x y', real=True)\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(x + 3*y, 3)\r\neq2 = Eq(abs(x) - abs(y), 1)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolutions = solve((eq1, eq2), (x, y))\r\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\nNotImplementedError: solving Abs(y) when the argument is not real or imaginary.\n```\nIt seems that solving the system of equations involving absolute values directly using `sympy` is causing issues. Instead, we can manually consider the different cases as we did earlier and verify that there are no valid solutions.\n\nFrom our earlier analysis, we identified the following cases and their corresponding systems of equations:\n\n1. \\(x \\geq 0\\) and \\(y \\geq 0\\): \\(x + 3y = 3\\) and \\(x - y = 1\\)\n2. \\(x \\geq 0\\) and \\(y \\leq 0\\): \\(x + 3y = 3\\) and \\(x + y = 1\\)\n3. \\(x \\leq 0\\) and \\(y \\geq 0\\): \\(x + 3y = 3\\) and \\(-x + y = 1\\)\n4. \\(x \\leq 0\\) and \\(y \\leq 0\\): \\(-x + y = 1\\) and \\(-x - y = 1\\)\n\nSince we found that none of these cases led to valid solutions, we can conclude that the system of equations has no solutions.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
